 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) Terrain Density](17156-fractal-brownian-motion-fbm-terrain-density.md)
+Generates 3D terrain density fields by summing multiple octaves of simplex noise, modified by a base surface plane, terracing, and bedrock layers. It differs from standard value-noise FBM by transforming the raw noise output into peaks at zero-crossings and valleys at extrema, creating distinct topological features suitable for voxel-based terrain generation.
+
 ### CPU Value-Noise FBM World Generation
-A dependency-free CPU implementation that generates per-body heightfields and cave structures by writing directly into a packed voxel array. It uses a deterministic seed and a shared BiomeProfile to produce distinct worlds per celestial body. Unlike the GPU-oriented texture generation, this method operates on sparse voxel grids and integrates with chunk-based streaming systems [S2, unofficial].
+A dependency-free CPU value-noise FBM generates a per-body heightfield and cave structures, writing directly into the packed voxel array [S2, unofficial]. Generation is derived from a shared BiomeProfile to produce distinct worlds per body, using a deterministic seed [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### CloudDome Volume Texture Generation
-Generates 3D volume noise textures for atmospheric cloud domes. It uses 6 octaves, initial frequency 6, initial amplitude 0.7, and persistence 0.5. The default resolution is 128 to balance memory and visual quality. The output is stored in DDS format with only the ALPHA channel used to minimize size. Due to driver limitations with `gluBuild3DMipMapLevels`, mipmaps are disabled, and LINEAR filtering is used for both minification and magnification [S3, unofficial].
+Generates volume noise texture using NoiseGenerator with 6 octaves, initial frequency 6, initial amplitude 0.7, and persistence 0.5 if no valid DDS file is provided [S3, unofficial]. The default resolution is 128, a compromise between memory and visual appearance [S3, unofficial]. The image format is DDS with only the ALPHA channel to keep size small [S3, unofficial]. Texture filtering is set to LINEAR because DDS format does not support volume texture mipmaps, and `gluBuild3DMipMapLevels` is not fully supported by all drivers [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N^2 \cdot \text{octaves})$ for 2D, $O(N^3 \cdot \text{octaves})$ for 3D, where $N$ is resolution. Each sample requires bilinear interpolation and smoothstep evaluation [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^2 \cdot \text{octaves})$ for grid storage if all octaves are kept in memory, or $O(N^2)$ if grids are generated and discarded per octave. The output texture is $O(N^2)$ or $O(N^3)$ [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** On a 256x256 2D texture, generation is fast enough for runtime initialization. For 3D volumes at 128^3, generation time is significant but acceptable for pre-generation or low-frequency updates [S3, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Seamless tiling, smooth gradients, low memory footprint compared to gradient noise (no vector storage), easy to implement on CPU or GPU [S1, S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Less directional control than Perlin noise; smoothstep interpolation can introduce slight blurring at high frequencies; 3D mipmapping support is limited in standard formats [S3, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Cloud textures, terrain heightmaps, procedural materials requiring smooth, organic patterns.
-- **When Not to Use:** When sharp, directional features are needed (use Perlin or Worley); when high-frequency detail is critical without blurring (use higher octaves or gradient noise).
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(O \cdot N^2)$ for 2D textures and $O(O \cdot N^3)$ for 3D volumes, where $O$ is the number of octaves and $N$ is the resolution [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^2)$ for 2D output and $O(N^3)$ for 3D output, plus $O(O \cdot C^2)$ for intermediate grids where $C$ is the cell count per octave [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Value noise is generally faster than gradient noise (Perlin) because it avoids dot products and gradient vector storage, relying only on scalar interpolation [S1, unofficial]. However, it produces smoother, less detailed textures [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Deterministic, easy to implement, smooth results suitable for clouds, low memory footprint for intermediate grids [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Less directional detail than Perlin noise; 3D volume textures may suffer from lack of mipmap support in some formats/drivers (DDS) [S3, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Cloud rendering, terrain heightmaps, procedural textures where smoothness is preferred over sharp detail [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When high-frequency directional detail is required (use Perlin/Simplex) or when mipmap support is critical for 3D volumes and the format does not support it [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Value-Noise FBM was popularized in procedural texture generation for computer graphics, particularly in the context of cloud rendering and terrain synthesis. The specific implementation using LCG-based PRNG and bilinear interpolation with smoothstep is documented in early voxel game development and atmospheric simulation research [S1, S3, unofficial]. The method is a staple in real-time graphics due to its balance of visual quality and computational efficiency [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Value-Noise FBM was popularized in procedural texture generation for computer graphics, particularly for cloud simulation. The specific implementation details cited here originate from voxel game development and early GPU-based cloud simulation research [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The use of value noise for clouds allows for efficient runtime generation on both CPU and GPU, with the FBM structure providing the necessary fractal detail to mimic natural cloud formations [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
